contents.text:
This program starts the coding agent in the terminal.

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt].
// It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(), new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance, Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The `using` lines bring in the framework's namespaces: web access, the three model providers, telemetry, the hosting types, the terminal host, and the coding demo. The last line is the whole program. `CliHost.RunAsync` takes the command-line arguments, so one prompt can run from the shell. It takes `CodingCatalog.Create()`, which names the identities and packs the agent loads. It takes `HostServices`, which lends the agent web access, telemetry and the three model providers. `CliHost` lives in `LouisAgent.Cli`, as the Cli project doc says.

To run it, set one model. For a local model, use `LLM_PROFILE=ollama`. For Anthropic, put your key in `.env.secrets`.

```
LLM_PROFILE=ollama
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-key-here
```

Your own agent keeps this shape. You change the catalogue you pass in, so it names your identities and packs.

